<!--
    Kuna GH-1661: a bit-test guard above two RV64 tail calls panicked
    jump-table recovery with a shift overflow.

    `slli a1,a0,0x23; bltz a1` tests bit 28 of a 32-bit argument, so the
    shifted value's non-zero mask is 0xfffffff800000000: its lowest set bit
    is 35.  Each `auipc t1; jr -0x18(t1)` tail call is a computed jump, and
    its guard analysis pulls the branch condition back through the shift and
    intersects it with the range that mask describes.  CircleRange::setNZMask
    builds that range with the stride 1 << 35, which the 32-bit step cannot
    hold: a debug build panicked, a release build wrapped the stride to 8.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 range fabric (`CircleRange::set_nz_mask`), consumed
    by P2 jump-table guard analysis (`circlerange_pull_back`).  A strict
    correctness fix: no option, one pass.  A mask whose stride does not fit
    the step forms no range and the guard keeps the range it had.

    Source (rustc -O, riscv64gc-unknown-none-elf, the .text bytes of a, b and
    pick verbatim below):

        pub extern "C" fn a(x: u32) -> u32 { read_volatile(x as *const u32) }
        pub extern "C" fn b(x: u32) -> u32 { read_volatile((x + 8) as *const u32) }
        pub extern "C" fn pick(x: u32) -> u32 {
            if x & (1 << 28) != 0 { b(x) } else { a(x) }
        }

    #1 proves pick decompiles (the bit test survives as the guard); #2-#3
    prove both tail-call destinations are reached, one per arm.
-->
